Wrong premise. Fix it before you write a line of code: the output is NOT JSON. Input is one JSON object read from stdin; output is raw text written to stdout — no envelope, no wrapper object, no PASS/FAIL metadata. The 'op' field routes, and the four valid values are exactly: 'minify', 'decode_bytes', 'middleware_response', 'view_request'. Here are the first three contracts, verbatim from the public case files.

Point 1 — op='minify'. Input fields: 'html' (required string), 'ignore_comments' (optional, default true — which matches the golden core signature def minify(html: str, ignore_comments: bool = True) -> str). Output: the minified HTML plus a trailing newline. Contract file:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "html": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "op": "minify"
      },
      "expected_output": "<html><head></head><body>some text here </body></html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "html": "<html>\n    <body>I selected you!</body>\n    </html>",
        "op": "minify"
      },
      "expected_output": "<html><head></head><body>I selected you!</body></html>\n"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Collapse runs of insignificant whitespace between and inside tags into a single space, while normalizing the document into a single line; the parser also inserts an empty head when one is absent."
}

Point 2 — op='decode_bytes'. Input fields: 'bytes_base64' (required), 'encoding' (optional preferred-encoding override). Output: 'decoded=<text>\n'. Contract file:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "bytes_base64": "QmzDoSBibMOh",
        "op": "decode_bytes"
      },
      "expected_output": "decoded=Blá blá\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "bytes_base64": "QmzhIGJs4Q==",
        "op": "decode_bytes"
      },
      "expected_output": "decoded=Blá blá\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "encoding": "IBM857",
        "bytes_base64": "QmygIGJsoA==",
        "op": "decode_bytes"
      },
      "expected_output": "decoded=Blá blá\n"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Decode a raw byte string into text by trying a preferred encoding first and falling back through alternatives, returning the recovered text; the caller may override the preferred encoding."
}

Point 3 — op='middleware_response'. Input fields: 'content', 'status_code'; 'content_type' optional (defaults to text/html); additional optional gating fields ('path', 'exclude_paths', 'minify_response', 'keep_comments', 'minify_enabled', 'debug', 'hit') are exercised by separate case files — ask per feature when you need them. Output format: 'status_code=<n>\ncontent_type=<ct>\n---content---\n' followed by the final body plus a trailing newline. Contract file:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "status_code": 301,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=301\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "status_code": 200,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html><head></head><body>some text here </body></html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "content_type": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html; charset=utf-8\n---content---\n<html><head></head><body>some text here </body></html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "content_type": "application/json",
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=application/json\n---content---\n<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>\n"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Only post-process responses that return a success status and an HTML media type; any other status code or media type passes the body through unchanged. A charset suffix on the media type still counts as HTML."
}

Too many questions. Please digest these three first, and come back for alignment after organizing the subsequent architecture — 'view_request' waits for your next round.

Three points, then you're on your own.

Point 1 — The view_request contract. Input: a single field 'path'. Output format: 'status_code=<n>\nminify_response=<unset|false>\n---content---\n' followed by the final body. Note the second line reports the flag state, not a content type. Public contract file feature4_view_decorators, verbatim:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "path": "/min",
        "op": "view_request"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\nminify_response=unset\n---content---\n<html><head></head><body><p>Hello world! :D</p><div>Copyright 3000</div></body></html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "path": "/not_min",
        "op": "view_request"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\nminify_response=false\n---content---\n\n<html>\n    <body>\n        <p>Hello world! :D</p>\n        <div>Copyright 3000</div>\n    </body>\n</html>\n    \n"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Expose per-view control over minification through the HTTP layer: one route is wrapped so its rendered HTML is minified, another is wrapped to mark its response as non-minifiable (carrying an opt-out flag) and leaves the body untouched."
}

Point 2 — Opt-in/opt-out mechanics. A per-response minify_response flag is the mechanism: set to false it explicitly opts that response out (body passes through unchanged); set to true it opts in. When unset, the middleware's global eligibility rules apply. The opt-out decorator sets the flag to false WITHOUT touching the body; the opt-in decorator minifies the body directly and leaves the flag unset — which is exactly why /min reports minify_response=unset yet still emits collapsed markup.

Point 3 — Interaction with the global toggle, debug, keep_comments, exclusions. For responses carrying an unset flag that flow through middleware gating, the global rules govern: minify_enabled=false disables regardless of everything; when minify_enabled is null/unset, debug=true disables and debug=false/unset enables. As for keep_comments and path exclusions: those fields are NOT part of the view_request contract — its input is 'path' only. The opt-in decorator minifies the body directly rather than re-entering middleware gating, and the opt-out decorator short-circuits before any of it matters. Don't invent extra fields for this op.

That's five questions in one breath. Three is my limit. Here are the first three that hit the spec.

Point 1 — path/exclude_paths. Yes: patterns are regular expressions matched against the request path with any leading slash stripped. So the pattern ^raw matches the path /raw/ because matching is performed against raw/. When no exclusion list is configured, eligible responses are minified. Public contract file feature3_2_url_exclusion, verbatim:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "path": "/raw/",
        "exclude_paths": [
          "^raw"
        ],
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "path": "/",
        "exclude_paths": null,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html><head></head><body>some text here </body></html>\n"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Skip minification for request paths matching the configured exclusion patterns (matched against the path with any leading slash removed); when no exclusion list is configured, eligible responses are minified."
}

Point 2 — minify_response. Three states. Set to false: the response is explicitly opted out, body passes through unchanged. Set to true: opted in. Unset (null/absent): the middleware's global eligibility rules decide. Mechanically, the opt-out decorator sets the flag to false without touching the body; the opt-in decorator minifies the body directly and leaves the flag unset.

Point 3 — minify_enabled/debug. When minify_enabled is explicitly false, minification is off regardless of everything else. When minify_enabled is null/unset, debug controls it: debug=true disables, debug=false or unset enables. So null minify_enabled + debug=true means no minification. Public contract file feature3_5_global_toggle, verbatim:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "minify_enabled": false,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "debug": true,
        "minify_enabled": null,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "debug": false,
        "minify_enabled": null,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html><head></head><body>some text here </body></html>\n"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Respect the global minification switch: when explicitly disabled the body is untouched; when unset, debug mode disables minification while non-debug mode enables it."
}

Too many questions. Please digest these three first, and come back for alignment after organizing the subsequent architecture — keep_comments, the hit flag, and the precedence question wait for your next round.

Exactly three. Here they are.

Point 1 — keep_comments. Semantics per the feature3_4 contract: when comment retention is enabled (keep_comments=true) the comment survives minification; when it is disabled OR unspecified/absent, the comment is stripped. So the default when the field is absent is strip. Mapping to the core: the middleware layer translates this into the golden core parameter — keep_comments=true means calling the core with ignore_comments=false; absent/false means ignore_comments=true (the default). Public contract file feature3_4_comment_retention, verbatim:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <!-- some comment --><body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "keep_comments": true,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html><!-- some comment --><head></head><body>some text here </body></html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <!-- some comment --><body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "keep_comments": false,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html><head></head><body>some text here </body></html>\n"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Control comment handling globally: when comment retention is enabled the comment survives minification; when it is disabled or unspecified the comment is stripped."
}

Point 2 — hit. The hit flag indicates whether the request actually passed through the request phase of the middleware. Values: true/false. If hit is false, the response body is left unchanged regardless of ALL other settings — no status check, no toggle check, nothing rescues it. Only when hit is true does normal minification eligibility logic apply. Public contract file feature3_6_request_eligibility, verbatim:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "hit": true,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html><head></head><body>some text here </body></html>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "content": "<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>",
        "hit": false,
        "op": "middleware_response"
      },
      "expected_output": "status_code=200\ncontent_type=text/html\n---content---\n<html>   <body>some text here</body>    </html>\n"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Only minify responses for requests that passed through the request phase of the middleware; a request that never hit the middleware leaves its response unchanged."
}

Point 3 — precedence. Stop looking for a secret ordering; the spec defines these as independent short-circuit gates that AND together. Minification happens only if every gate passes; any single failing gate leaves the body byte-identical. The full gate set for middleware_response: (1) hit must be true — false kills it regardless of everything else; (2) minify_enabled must not be explicitly false — that wording is 'regardless of other settings', so it beats even minify_response=true; (3) if minify_enabled is null/unset, debug must not be true; (4) minify_response must not be false; (5) status code must be 2xx; (6) content type must start with text/html (charset suffix allowed); (7) request path must not match any exclusion regex (matched with leading slash stripped). Because one failure suffices and failures are pass-through, the relative evaluation order among these gates is unobservable in output — implement them as a chain of early returns in any order, but never mutate the body before all gates have passed.

Two questions, one of them only partially on file. Here is what I hold.

Point 1 — decode_bytes. On file: the helper tries the preferred encoding first (defaulting to UTF-8), then falls back through a fixed sequence of alternative encodings — including latin-1 / ISO-8859-1 style single-byte encodings — until one succeeds. The caller overrides the preferred encoding via the 'encoding' field (e.g. IBM857), and a supplied preferred encoding is tried FIRST — the IBM857 case in the contract file only decodes correctly under that ordering. Output: 'decoded=<text>\n'. Public contract file feature2_decode_bytes, verbatim:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "bytes_base64": "QmzDoSBibMOh",
        "op": "decode_bytes"
      },
      "expected_output": "decoded=Blá blá\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "bytes_base64": "QmzhIGJs4Q==",
        "op": "decode_bytes"
      },
      "expected_output": "decoded=Blá blá\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "encoding": "IBM857",
        "bytes_base64": "QmygIGJsoA==",
        "op": "decode_bytes"
      },
      "expected_output": "decoded=Blá blá\n"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Decode a raw byte string into text by trying a preferred encoding first and falling back through alternatives, returning the recovered text; the caller may override the preferred encoding."
}

What is NOT on file: the exact ordered list of the alternative encodings beyond 'a fixed sequence including latin-1-style ones', and the behavior when every encoding fails. I will not fabricate either. For those two aspects, my answer, verbatim: That's a good question but I don't have specific details on that aspect. You'll want to look at the existing test cases in rcb_tests/public_test_cases/ and the PRD in start.md for the precise expected behavior — the test JSON files are the authoritative contract for the adapter's input/output format.

Point 2 — Protected regions. Exactly three element types: pre, script, and textarea. Their inner content is preserved byte-for-byte — whitespace, indentation, and newlines inside them are NOT touched, not collapsed, period (see the second feature1_4 case where the textarea body keeps its blank line intact). Two boundary caveats: attribute serialization on the protected opening tags themselves is still normalized — attributes sorted alphabetically (textarea cols, id, name, rows) — and any HTML appearing inside such regions is escaped to entities by the parsing step. There is no fourth protected tag; don't go hunting for one.

Correction before I hand anything over: feature1_6 is the encoding/Unicode file, not 'CDN script'. The CDN script case lives in feature1_4_protected_regions, which I already disclosed earlier in this thread. I output feature1_6 by its number, as asked. Three full files, verbatim.

File 1 — feature1_2_linebreaks:

{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "html": "<html><body>Click <a href=\"#\">here</a>\nto see more</body></html>",
